@h1 Paragraphs & prose h1@

Prose is the ground floor of Wit. Plain text is already a valid document —
you type sentences, leave a blank line between paragraphs, and you are done.
Nothing else is required to write. Wit never hijacks your punctuation or the
way a line begins into structure, so you reach for markup only when you want a
specific element.

@h2 The one rule: a blank line starts a paragraph h2@

Write text. Separate two paragraphs with a blank line. That is the whole
mechanic for ordinary writing.

@@@pre
The rain had not stopped for three days.

Mara pressed her palm to the cold glass and counted
the boats that were no longer there.
pre@@@

Those two blocks become two paragraphs. One blank line or several — the result
is the same; extra blank lines never create empty paragraphs.

@h2 Soft line breaks vs. paragraph breaks h2@

A single line break inside a block is a _soft_ wrap: the newline is kept in the
source, but because HTML collapses whitespace, the sentence simply flows on with
a space. It does _not_ become a visible line break.

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken in eleven days,
and his silence had begun to feel like weather.
pre@@@

That renders as one flowing paragraph. Add a blank line between the two lines
instead and you get two paragraphs. That single extra newline is the only
difference between a wrapped sentence and a new paragraph.

@aside(class note) 💡 Need a hard line break inside a paragraph — for an address
block or a line of verse? Use the break node, covered in
@a |href /docs/write/inline-marks/| Inline marks a@. A lone newline will not do
it. aside@

@h2 Nothing is hijacked h2@

Wit has no Markdown-style block syntax. A line that opens with a number, a dash,
an asterisk, or a greater-than sign is just prose, and it renders verbatim:

@@@pre
> she said nothing at all.
* the asterisk keeps its place.
- and so does the hyphen.
1. The first time, it rained.
pre@@@

Each of those becomes its own literal paragraph — the leaders survive exactly
as typed (the greater-than sign is HTML-escaped for safety, but reads normally).
Because there is no bullet or blockquote block syntax to trip over, your
punctuation is always your own. When you want a real list or quotation, you ask
for it with a node — see @a |href /docs/write/lists/| Lists a@ and
@a |href /docs/write/quotes-code-rules/| Quotes, code & rules a@.

@h2 What counts as a blank line h2@

A "blank line" is a line that is empty _or_ contains only spaces and tabs. A
line of pure whitespace still separates paragraphs — it will not silently merge
two blocks. This matters when you copy text between editors that leave trailing
spaces on "empty" lines: the split still works as you expect.

@h2 Whitespace, length, and line endings h2@

Wit is forgiving about the mechanical details of a text file, so you rarely
think about them:

@ul
@li _Leading whitespace is cosmetic._ Indenting a paragraph with spaces or tabs is
preserved in the source but collapsed by the browser, so indented prose reads
normally. Use indentation freely to keep your source tidy. li@
@li _Line length carries no meaning._ A single 500-character line is one
paragraph; there is no column limit and no need to hard-wrap. li@
@li _Line endings are normalised._ Windows (CRLF), classic-Mac (CR), Unix (LF),
or a mix in one file all normalise before paragraphs are split, so a file
authored on any platform renders identically. li@
@li _File edges are forgiving._ An empty file is an empty document, not an error.
A missing trailing newline, or several trailing newlines, both render the same
single paragraph — no phantom empty paragraphs appear. li@
ul@

@h2 Punctuation and special characters h2@

Ordinary punctuation is never reserved. Em-dashes, apostrophes, colons,
semicolons, quotation marks, ampersands, and percent signs all render as
themselves (with the few HTML-sensitive characters escaped for you):

@@@pre
"It was a matter of degree," she said — half to herself, half to the sea.
Costs rose 40% between 1847 and 1851; nobody noticed.
pre@@@

Quotation marks round-trip verbatim — Wit does _not_ substitute "smart" curly
quotes. What you type is what you get. Numbers that happen to look like syntax
stay literal too: a year followed by a period (@code 1970. code@), a decimal
(@code 3.14 code@), and arithmetic shapes (@code 5*6*7 code@) are all plain prose.

@h2 Three characters to keep off your prose h2@

Three characters carry meaning at the edges of words, and a bare one glued to a
letter can be misread as markup. None of them has a backslash escape in running
prose, so the fix is always to reword or to wrap the mention in inline code.

@h3 A literal at-sign glued to letters h3@

An at-sign directly against letters is read as a node boundary. An email address
in the middle of a sentence does _not_ survive:

@@@pre
Email me at keeper@example.org today.
pre@@@

renders as two broken paragraphs — the address is mangled, because
@@@code keeper@ code@@@ is read as a node close. Wrapping it in
@@@code @code … code@ code@@@ does not rescue it either. There is no backslash
escape for the at-sign in prose.

@aside(class note) 💡 The fix: keep a bare at-sign off a following letter.
Rephrase to "keeper at example.org", split the address so no word-at-word pair
sits in the text, or put the address in a link's @code href code@ parameter (see
@a |href /docs/write/links-media/| Links & media a@). An at-sign before a _space_
or a _digit_ is always safe — "Meet me @ home", "in @2024" — so only the
letter-glued case is a hazard. aside@

@h3 A hash glued to a letter at the start of a line h3@

A hash pressed against a letter at the start of a line begins a _definition_ and
swallows the line — so a line that opens with a word-tag renders as nothing.
Keep a hash off a following letter: a hash before a space or a digit is fine.
When you need to write about a hash-tag or a sharp-key like C-sharp in prose,
wrap the mention in inline code.

@h3 A bare pipe in running text h3@

A bare vertical bar opens parameter state and is swallowed, so a sentence that
puts bars between three numbers loses them. Avoid a bare pipe in running prose;
if you must show one, put it inside frozen inline code.

@h2 A tilde attached to a token is prose h2@

A tilde is only special as a comment marker when it leads a line with a space
after it (or as the doubled comment fence). Glued to a token it is ordinary
text, so shell paths and approximations are safe: a home path like
@code ~/Documents code@ and an approximation like @code ~6 hours code@ both stay
literal. Comments have their own page.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Wrapping prose in a paragraph node._ Prose auto-paragraphs, so an explicit
@@@code @p … p@ code@@@ around a sentence double-wraps it into
@code <p><p>…</p></p> code@. Only reach for the paragraph node when a container
needs a paragraph you can target or attribute. li@
@li _Trusting an email or a raw pipe in prose._ Both break silently (above).
Reword, or move the value into a parameter. li@
@li _Expecting a hard line break from a wrapped line._ A lone newline is a soft
break that reads as a space; use the break node for a real one. li@
ul@

